WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.587
Firmware often accesses hardware through
memory-mapped registers,

2
00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:06.232
peripheral access crates, hardware
abstraction layers,

3
00:00:06.312 --> 00:00:09.504
board support packages, drivers, and
device-specific configuration.

4
00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:13.142
These layers are where Rust code meets
behavior the compiler cannot fully model.

5
00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:15.106
Memory-mapped I/O is not ordinary memory.

6
00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:18.430
Register reads and writes may acknowledge
interrupts, start transfers,

7
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.859
clear flags, change power state, or affect
hardware outside the processor.

8
00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:25.184
Initialization order, clock configuration,
pin modes, reset behavior,

9
00:00:25.264 --> 00:00:28.560
interrupt setup, and peripheral ownership
all influence safe operation.

10
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.408
Some hardware-facing code requires
`unsafe` because Rust cannot prove the validity

11
00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:35.105
of hardware side effects, raw addresses,
volatile access,

12
00:00:35.185 --> 00:00:37.016
or aliasing rules at the device boundary.

13
00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:38.561
That does not make the code wrong.

14
00:00:38.641 --> 00:00:41.886
It makes the boundary important enough to
document and review carefully.

15
00:00:41.966 --> 00:00:44.268
Safe wrappers should protect hardware
invariants.

16
00:00:44.348 --> 00:00:47.017
They should make invalid operation order
harder to express,

17
00:00:47.097 --> 00:00:50.498
prevent unrelated code from controlling
the same peripheral unexpectedly,

18
00:00:50.578 --> 00:00:53.613
and hide low-level details behind APIs
that communicate ownership,

19
00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:55.787
initialization state, and valid
transitions.

20
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:59.320
Unsafe code should be small, justified,
documented, tested where practical,

21
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.383
and revisited when hardware, platform
crates, board support code,

22
00:01:02.463 --> 00:01:05.393
generated register definitions, or
toolchain behavior changes.

23
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:08.927
A safety contract should explain why
assumptions hold and how safe callers are

24
00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:10.420
prevented from violating them.
